139533354 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] [17592186093370 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like\n\n$$\ny = \\ln (2x+1)\n$$\n\nFirst notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{du}(\\ln u) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(2x + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{u} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{u}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nu=2x+1\n$$\n\nwe obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2}{2x + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417 false] [17592186093370 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like\n\n$$\ny = \\ln (2x+1)\n$$\n\nFirst notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{dy}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{dx} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{du}(\\ln u) \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(2x + 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{u} \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{u}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nu=2x+1\n$$\n\nwe obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{2}{2x + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533354 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] [17592186093370 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like \n\n$$\ny = \\ln (2x+1).\n$$\n\nFirst notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x+1,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535680 #edb/ref 17592186045417 false] [17592186093370 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like \n\n$$\ny = \\ln (2x+1).\n$$\n\nFirst notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x+1,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139533438 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] [17592186093370 #edb/ref 1064 "The chain rule can be used to differentiate logarithmic functions like \n\n$$\ny = \\ln (2x+1).\n$$\n\nFirst notice that this function can be written as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\ln u, \\qquad u = 2x+1.\n$$\n\nWe can use the chain rule to compute the derivative of this composite function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡u)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=2x+1,$ we obtain the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{2x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 13194139535680 #edb/ref 1759218604